<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chūgoku region</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chugoku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chūgoku region is the western end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, the main island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, made up of the prefectures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a>, Shimane, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori">Tottori</a> and Yamaguchi. Its mountains divide a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> coast from the Inland Sea coast. In the 16th century the Mōri clan conquered most of the region, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> it was divided among domains such as Chōshū, Hiroshima, Okayama and Tottori.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chugoku-region.jpg" alt="Chūgoku region" width="320" height="290" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chugoku_Region_in_Japan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
